WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.320
Local attack surface is the set of services, software, interfaces, accounts,

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:08.660
and configurations that could be misused or fail under pressure.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.360
Reducing it means removing or restricting what the system does not need.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:18.780
Safe examples include uninstalling unused software, disabling unnecessary services,

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:22.920
closing unneeded local listening ports, restricting remote access,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:25.620
reviewing default or unused accounts,

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:30.000
limiting autorun behavior, and avoiding tools that have no business purpose.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:33.420
Remote access deserves deliberate review.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.280
A system may need remote administration, remote support, or application connectivity,

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:42.980
but those paths should be intentional, authenticated, logged, and scoped.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.040
Broad remote access created for convenience can become long-term risk.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:52.060
If a remote access method is required, document why it exists, who owns it,

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:55.080
what controls apply, and how it is monitored.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.860
Account cleanup is also attack surface reduction.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:02.320
Default accounts, stale local users, old administrator accounts,

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:06.780
unmanaged service accounts, and test accounts can create confusion and risk.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.600
Review them with the system owner before removal or restriction.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:15.620
Some accounts exist for maintenance or recovery, so safe hardening requires understanding,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.400
approval, and a rollback path.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:20.300
Production safety matters.

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.320
Do not remove software, disable services, or change access paths blindly.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.260
Confirm business purpose, test with a pilot group,

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.440
choose a maintenance window when needed, back up configuration, document the change,

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:35.020
and define rollback.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.800
Good hardening removes unnecessary exposure while preserving the required function of the

00:01:40.020 --> 00:01:40.900
system.
